# Flags the Flatpak relay unit adds. `EnvironmentFile=-%h/.config/suite-sync/relay.env` in
# `deploy/suite-sync-flatpak.service` makes this file optional; keep the line empty for loopback.
#
# The unit already starts the relay on 127.0.0.1 with the store in the app's data directory, so
# this file only adds what a reachable relay needs: an address, the acknowledgement, and a
# certificate pair inside the sandbox (nothing else is writable there).
#
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes \
#     -keyout $HOME/.var/app/run.suite.sync/data/tls/relay-key.pem \
#     -out $HOME/.var/app/run.suite.sync/data/tls/relay-cert.pem -days 3650 \
#     -subj "/CN=suite-sync relay"
#   chmod 600 $HOME/.var/app/run.suite.sync/data/tls/relay-key.pem
#
# A systemd environment file does not expand `$HOME` or quotes, so write the absolute path of
# this account. systemd splits the value on whitespace; a path with spaces is not supported here.
#
#   SUITE_SYNC_ARGS=--trust-all-remote --host 0.0.0.0 --tls-cert /home/you/.var/app/run.suite.sync/data/tls/relay-cert.pem --tls-key /home/you/.var/app/run.suite.sync/data/tls/relay-key.pem
#
# `--trust-all-remote` on `0.0.0.0` admits every device that can route to the port: any of them
# can join and write frames and membership, and the relay does not verify the role a record claims.
# Read docs/how-to/relay.md first. The relay prints the certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint at
# startup; each device pins that line, so keep the pair stable until you replace it deliberately.
